1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:09,620
It began as a toy over 500 years ago, but has evolved into one of the most versatile aircraft in the world.

2
00:00:10,620 --> 00:00:16,600
The machine that Wilbur Wright once thought worthless has become an indispensable part of our world.

3
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:20,880
Now, helicopters on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:42,540
In watching a premier military attack helicopter in action,

5
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:49,600
it's no wonder that many of the world's aeronauts claim that helicopters are the only real flying machines.

6
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:57,320
The craft that became known as the Angel of Mercy in war has become the jack of all trades of our airways.

7
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,280
From a transporter of some of the world's most famous and powerful individuals.

8
00:01:07,720 --> 00:01:12,340
To aerial firefighters putting out some of our worst forest fires.

9
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:17,400
From defender and protector of our neighborhoods.

10
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:22,380
To the flying crane workhorses lifting over 12 tons.

11
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:28,780
To one of the most important functions of this aircraft, the flying ambulance.

12
00:01:28,781 --> 00:01:32,780
Medevac helicopters have helped save thousands of lives a year.

13
00:01:34,780 --> 00:01:41,780
Yet less than 60 years ago, the only aircraft in the world that flies in three dimensions was still in its infancy.

14
00:01:47,780 --> 00:01:51,780
The idea of rotary flight dates back to ancient civilization.

15
00:01:52,780 --> 00:01:55,780
It began with a toy that became known as the Chinese top.

16
00:01:55,781 --> 00:01:56,794
Chinese top.

17
00:01:56,795 --> 00:02:03,720
We know for a fact that 1,500 to 2,000 years ago, the Chinese already had spin top

18
00:02:03,721 --> 00:02:08,661
toys where you pulled a string and a propeller device flew into the air.

19
00:02:08,662 --> 00:02:10,240
The implications of it,

20
00:02:10,241 --> 00:02:13,649
however, were not realized by the Chinese at that time.

21
00:02:13,650 --> 00:02:16,500
As the Chinese top made its way across Europe,

22
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:19,297
it quickly became a popular curio.

23
00:02:19,298 --> 00:02:23,300
By 1463, the top even began to show up in paintings,

24
00:02:23,301 --> 00:02:28,620
such as the famous Christ Child, which depicts Jesus holding one of these toys.

25
00:02:31,120 --> 00:02:37,300
Shortly after, in 1483, the first known design concept of a helicopter was put on paper

26
00:02:37,301 --> 00:02:40,180
by one of the world's most famous theorists.

27
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:46,520
Leonardo da Vinci was very actively pursuing the challenge of the helicopter

28
00:02:46,521 --> 00:02:51,400
with that famous sketch of his and with the description that if the cloth were properly varnished

29
00:02:51,401 --> 00:02:54,800
and this screw-like object were turned quickly enough,

30
00:02:55,160 --> 00:02:56,960
it would ascend into the air.

31
00:02:57,320 --> 00:02:59,460
And if you look back at it, fascinating,

32
00:03:00,060 --> 00:03:05,360
how close Leonardo da Vinci was to solving the problem of flight.

33
00:03:05,680 --> 00:03:09,900
But the man was such a perfectionist that he went beyond the glider,

34
00:03:10,540 --> 00:03:13,680
and if he had just stopped for a moment and said,

35
00:03:13,681 --> 00:03:16,740
OK, I don't think I can build this,

36
00:03:16,880 --> 00:03:19,080
but if I just make it into a fixed wing,

37
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:20,780
that man would have flown.

38
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:23,400
500 years ago.

39
00:03:23,401 --> 00:03:30,400
Da Vinci was among many scientists who were teased by maple seeds and dandelion leaves in flight.

40
00:03:30,401 --> 00:03:35,400
Various insects and hummingbirds who hovered effortlessly in mid-air,

41
00:03:35,401 --> 00:03:40,400
turned any which way they pleased and could fly in all directions.

42
00:03:40,401 --> 00:03:44,400
But without a sufficient source of power to lift a man,

43
00:03:44,401 --> 00:03:50,400
the idea of any mechanical flight would remain for centuries a dream.

44
00:03:51,400 --> 00:04:00,520
By the turn of the 19th century, many visionaries had put their ideas of a rotary aircraft on paper.

45
00:04:01,120 --> 00:04:04,080
However, most were based on fantasy rather than reality.

46
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:08,540
But by the 1840s, an English scientist, Sir George Cayley,

47
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:15,240
began tackling one of the first obstacles associated with rotary flight, counteracting torque.

48
00:04:15,980 --> 00:04:20,820
If you take a hold of something, a screwdriver, for instance, and you twist it in one direction,

49
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:26,200
there is an equal and opposite reaction in the opposite direction.

50
00:04:27,420 --> 00:04:30,100
And that would be the torque reaction.

51
00:04:30,980 --> 00:04:34,160
And when a rotor is spinning in the air,

52
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:36,820
at the same time it is turning in one direction,

53
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:40,140
it's trying to twist the fuselage of the aircraft in the opposite direction.

54
00:04:41,440 --> 00:04:46,480
Cayley's answer to this problem was to design a helicopter with two rotors side by side.

55
00:04:46,481 --> 00:04:52,740
each rotor would turn in opposite directions to counteract the forces of torque between each other.

56
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,900
But Cayley never built his rotary aircraft.

57
00:04:57,420 --> 00:05:02,580
He knew that a full-size machine based on his design would never get off the ground.

58
00:05:02,581 --> 00:05:05,700
See, the only power at that time really was a steam engine.

59
00:05:06,520 --> 00:05:09,320
And steam engine weighs about 100 pounds per horsepower.

60
00:05:09,500 --> 00:05:11,800
It's just impractical for vertical lift.

61
00:05:11,801 --> 00:05:19,100
But several years later in the 1860s, a French engineer, Viscount Gustave de Ponton d'Amacourt,

62
00:05:19,440 --> 00:05:24,140
was determined to prove to the world that rotary flight was indeed possible.

63
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:31,100
D'Amacourt built a miniature rotary aircraft which demonstrated a new method of counteracting torque.

64
00:05:33,020 --> 00:05:37,360
He researched and built small flying models,

65
00:05:37,361 --> 00:05:43,620
and one of them was a counter-rotating helicopter toy

66
00:05:43,621 --> 00:05:47,020
where he took a bow and would wind it up

67
00:05:47,021 --> 00:05:50,260
and the tension of the bow would turn one rotor in one direction.

68
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:52,760
The torque of that would turn the opposite rotor.

69
00:05:53,140 --> 00:05:56,080
Size may be two feet long, but it did fly.

70
00:05:56,820 --> 00:06:00,440
This became known as a coaxial method of handling torque.

71
00:06:01,160 --> 00:06:03,620
Two rotors mounted on a common axis.

72
00:06:03,621 --> 00:06:09,620
After proving that a properly designed coaxial rotor system would fly,

73
00:06:09,621 --> 00:06:13,620
de Amacor designed and built another miniature rotary aircraft.

74
00:06:13,621 --> 00:06:18,620
This time he utilized tiny watch spring mechanisms for power.

75
00:06:18,621 --> 00:06:20,620
They too flew successfully.

76
00:06:20,621 --> 00:06:25,620
De Amacor came up with an enduring name for his contrivances,

77
00:06:25,621 --> 00:06:30,620
using the Greek words helico, meaning spiral, and teron, meaning wing.

78
00:06:30,621 --> 00:06:33,824
wing. He coined the name Helicopter.

79
00:06:33,825 --> 00:06:38,720
But until a sufficient source of power became available

80
00:06:38,721 --> 00:06:44,787
enough to lift a man, helicopters would remain small, miniature prototypes.

81
00:06:44,788 --> 00:06:46,000
The world would

82
00:06:46,001 --> 00:06:51,310
have to wait another 20 years for vertical flight.

83
00:06:51,311 --> 00:06:55,240
In the 1880s, Thomas Edison attempted

84
00:06:55,241 --> 00:06:59,845
to build a helicopter. He created an engine powered by gun cotton.

85
00:06:59,846 --> 00:07:01,520
But after his laboratory

86
00:07:01,521 --> 00:07:06,800
blew up during one such experiment, he went back to doing what he knew best, developing

87
00:07:06,801 --> 00:07:12,540
electrical items. Helicopters will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

88
00:07:15,560 --> 00:07:21,440
By the mid-19th century, there were many failed attempts to design and build both fixed-wing

89
00:07:21,441 --> 00:07:23,660
and rotary-winged aircraft.

90
00:07:25,060 --> 00:07:28,080
But the failure of these attempts did not stem

91
00:07:28,081 --> 00:07:30,140
from a lack of aerodynamic knowledge.

92
00:07:31,260 --> 00:07:33,740
All of these early developments of aviation

93
00:07:33,741 --> 00:07:37,080
had to wait for the development of the internal combustion engine,

94
00:07:37,320 --> 00:07:38,040
the gasoline engine.

95
00:07:38,800 --> 00:07:42,300
Obviously, those people that were trying to fly with manpower,

96
00:07:42,301 --> 00:07:47,020
that is, by flapping their wings or pedaling a bicycle-like machine,

97
00:07:47,560 --> 00:07:49,320
were doomed to failure because of the fact

98
00:07:49,321 --> 00:07:51,460
that the human being cannot generate that amount of energy.

99
00:07:52,860 --> 00:07:57,480
But in 1876, the four-stroke internal combustion engine

100
00:07:57,481 --> 00:08:00,920
was perfected by Germany's Nikolaus Otto.

101
00:08:02,180 --> 00:08:03,900
By the turn of the 20th century,

102
00:08:04,060 --> 00:08:06,540
the piston engine's power-to-weight ratio

103
00:08:06,541 --> 00:08:09,800
was at levels that assured mechanical flight possible,

104
00:08:10,340 --> 00:08:11,600
at least with airplanes.

105
00:08:12,860 --> 00:08:16,980
On December 17, 1903, near Kitty Hawk, North Carolina,

106
00:08:17,840 --> 00:08:22,220
The Wright brothers made the first controlled, sustained airplane flights

107
00:08:22,221 --> 00:08:25,120
in a powered, heavier-than-air vehicle.

108
00:08:26,620 --> 00:08:30,600
Now, the Wright engine was about 15 pounds per horsepower,

109
00:08:30,900 --> 00:08:35,280
but that was possible for an airplane, but really not for a helicopter.

110
00:08:35,740 --> 00:08:40,020
So when the weight of the engine, reciprocating engine,

111
00:08:40,021 --> 00:08:42,640
got down to around 3 pounds per horsepower,

112
00:08:42,820 --> 00:08:45,900
that's when vertical lift machines would be possible.

113
00:08:46,980 --> 00:08:55,980
On September 29th 1907, French aviation pioneer Louis Breguet became the first to leave the ground vertically.

114
00:08:55,981 --> 00:09:00,980
He and his craft hovered for one minute at an altitude of two feet.

115
00:09:00,981 --> 00:09:07,980
However, Breguet had no control of his gyroplane number one and his craft was tethered to the ground for safety.

116
00:09:07,981 --> 00:09:16,980
But on November 13th 1907 another French engineer Paul Cornu would also make aviation history.

117
00:09:16,981 --> 00:09:23,980
Hovering about a foot off the ground for several minutes Cornu became the first person to ever rise vertically

118
00:09:23,981 --> 00:09:28,980
in a powered heavier than air machine with no assistance from the ground.

119
00:09:28,981 --> 00:09:31,980
It was a true rotary free flight.

120
00:09:31,981 --> 00:09:35,110
But he too had little control of his machine.

121
00:09:35,111 --> 00:09:38,380
Although there were many engineers who followed

122
00:09:38,381 --> 00:09:44,380
in Cornu's footsteps, some even flying for up to seven minutes, they all had the same

123
00:09:44,381 --> 00:09:48,860
problem, lack of complete control.

124
00:09:48,861 --> 00:09:55,340
In contrast, by the 1920s, United States Army Air Service planes were making the first flights

125
00:09:55,341 --> 00:09:56,991
around the entire world.

126
00:09:56,992 --> 00:10:01,600
It seemed that helicopter technology had quickly fallen behind that

127
00:10:01,601 --> 00:10:03,783
of the airplane.

128
00:10:03,784 --> 00:10:13,060
In 1925, a Spaniard, Juan de la Sierra, put the idea of a helicopter

129
00:10:13,061 --> 00:10:15,627
back into mainstream development.

130
00:10:15,628 --> 00:10:20,140
His invention, a completely new type of aircraft, the auto

131
00:10:20,141 --> 00:10:20,841
gyro.

132
00:10:20,842 --> 00:10:28,720
Well, an auto gyro has a rotor and when the aircraft is moving forward through the

133
00:10:28,721 --> 00:10:34,260
air, the rotor acts a little bit like a windmill where the forward motion of the aircraft moving

134
00:10:34,261 --> 00:10:36,927
through the air causes it to rotate.

135
00:10:36,928 --> 00:10:40,780
And when it rotates, it again is a rotating wing and

136
00:10:40,781 --> 00:10:47,840
is producing lift. But the forward motion of the autogyro is produced by a propeller

137
00:10:47,841 --> 00:10:50,200
very similar to what you would have on an airplane.

138
00:10:50,201 --> 00:10:56,600
An autogyro, which wasn't really vertical lift on the takeoff, but vertical lift landing,

139
00:10:56,601 --> 00:11:03,100
They could auto-rotate down with very low ground run, plop down right in front of everybody,

140
00:11:03,380 --> 00:11:06,960
and especially at air shows, they would demonstrate that capability.

141
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:18,400
This method of landing is the same basic approach that a modern helicopter would take should its engine fail.

142
00:11:19,180 --> 00:11:26,200
Back in the 1920s, Juan de la Sierra proved to the world that to make a safe landing in a helicopter,

143
00:11:26,600 --> 00:11:29,340
required no engine power and no runway,

144
00:11:29,780 --> 00:11:32,500
just a spot big enough for the aircraft.

145
00:11:34,180 --> 00:11:37,440
The key to De La Sierva's stable aircraft

146
00:11:37,441 --> 00:11:39,400
was his discovery of a phenomenon

147
00:11:39,401 --> 00:11:43,140
that only became apparent with rotary-winged aircraft,

148
00:11:43,700 --> 00:11:45,040
dissimetry of lift.

149
00:11:46,180 --> 00:11:48,660
De La Sierva had the same annoying problem

150
00:11:48,661 --> 00:11:51,040
that many early helicopter designers had.

151
00:11:51,041 --> 00:11:54,380
Their craft would tip over before ever leaving the ground.

152
00:11:54,381 --> 00:11:58,540
This happened because most rotor blades at the time were rigid.

153
00:11:59,440 --> 00:12:04,460
Rotating blades never generate the same force of lift equally throughout their revolution.

154
00:12:05,060 --> 00:12:07,440
This causes an imbalance of lift.

155
00:12:10,460 --> 00:12:12,400
De La Sierva's solution?

156
00:12:13,140 --> 00:12:19,800
He attached hinges to each blade, which allowed them to move fore and aft, and up and down.

157
00:12:19,960 --> 00:12:23,820
This would allow the rotor blades to find their own way through the air.

158
00:12:24,380 --> 00:12:30,460
You see, as the forward-going blade goes into the air, it gets a lot more lift and it wants to rise.

159
00:12:30,880 --> 00:12:34,880
And as it rotates around and goes with the air, then it wants to sink.

160
00:12:35,660 --> 00:12:39,060
And prior to that, then, most of the aircraft would have rolled over.

161
00:12:39,620 --> 00:12:43,960
He allowed the blade to flap up and as it goes around, then, to flap down.

162
00:12:44,420 --> 00:12:46,380
If it gets too much lift, it flaps up.

163
00:12:46,660 --> 00:12:52,260
As it goes around and loses lift because it's going with the wind, it starts to settle.

164
00:12:52,261 --> 00:12:55,220
and so this was a self-accommodating sort of thing.

165
00:12:55,660 --> 00:12:57,900
So Wanda La Sierva figured that out

166
00:12:57,901 --> 00:13:03,520
and was able to build a controllable, flyable autogyro back in the 20s.

167
00:13:04,140 --> 00:13:08,140
Early autogyros had small wings to help steer the aircraft,

168
00:13:08,600 --> 00:13:11,620
but these wings disappeared in the early 1930s

169
00:13:11,621 --> 00:13:13,640
when cyclic pitch control was developed.

170
00:13:14,120 --> 00:13:18,240
This device was based on the problem that De La Sierva had just solved,

171
00:13:18,500 --> 00:13:19,540
dissymmetry of lift.

172
00:13:20,160 --> 00:13:25,140
The thought was take control of this imbalance of lift and use it to steer the aircraft.

173
00:13:26,720 --> 00:13:32,500
It wasn't until the cyclic pitch, where we can change the pitch as we go around the blade,

174
00:13:32,800 --> 00:13:35,420
so it might go high on this side and low on that side,

175
00:13:36,180 --> 00:13:40,380
that people began using cyclic pitch to help control this rotor.

176
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:45,220
And so then, in hovering, where you have no forward flight speed,

177
00:13:45,221 --> 00:13:49,640
they were able then to tilt the rotor so as to be able to go laterally

178
00:13:49,641 --> 00:13:52,600
or tilt the rotor so as to go longitudinally.

179
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:56,960
This is the same basic device that steers a modern helicopter.

180
00:13:57,600 --> 00:14:01,340
When the cyclic stick is moved, a device at the base of the rotor hub

181
00:14:01,341 --> 00:14:04,280
alters the pitch of each rotor blade individually

182
00:14:04,281 --> 00:14:06,700
as it goes through its cycle of rotation.

183
00:14:10,940 --> 00:14:13,020
So where the pitch in a blade is greater,

184
00:14:13,021 --> 00:14:15,814
it grabs more air and the blade rises higher.

185
00:14:15,815 --> 00:14:18,360
Where the pitch is less, the blade swings

186
00:14:18,361 --> 00:14:24,060
lower because it has less air to catch on its surface. The overall effect with this

187
00:14:24,061 --> 00:14:29,180
system is the change in the rotor plane, which enables directional control.

188
00:14:33,240 --> 00:14:37,106
Modern helicopters also have collective pitch control.

189
00:14:37,107 --> 00:14:39,540
Simply put, this lever changes the

190
00:14:39,541 --> 00:14:43,405
pitch of all the rotor blades evenly or collectively.

191
00:14:43,406 --> 00:14:46,540
This way the pilot can lift off vertically,

192
00:14:47,140 --> 00:14:53,140
steer the aircraft and with the cyclic stick compensate for those forces of unequal lift.

193
00:15:00,300 --> 00:15:06,300
Thanks to these new technological breakthroughs back in the 1930s, the Autogyro achieved practical

194
00:15:06,301 --> 00:15:13,136
success. The Detroit News used one to speed reporters to the scene of major stories.

195
00:15:13,137 --> 00:15:13,837
The

196
00:15:13,838 --> 00:15:17,756
U.S. Postal Service began test flights for its deliveries.

197
00:15:17,757 --> 00:15:20,020
There were even demonstrations

198
00:15:20,021 --> 00:15:25,340
held on the White House lawn for President Hoover, who called the auto gyro the greatest

199
00:15:25,341 --> 00:15:31,840
achievement in aviation. Harold Pitcairn, who was licensed by De La Sierra to build

200
00:15:31,841 --> 00:15:38,100
auto gyros in the United States even foresaw a future where auto gyros would make automobiles

201
00:15:38,101 --> 00:15:38,801
obsolete.

202
00:15:38,802 --> 00:15:44,040
He designed a Pitcairn roadable and demonstrated it on the streets of Washington,

203
00:15:44,260 --> 00:15:50,820
D.C. The roadable vehicle was all part of Pitcairn's dream, which he depicted in his

204
00:15:50,821 --> 00:15:56,304
promotional material, to have an auto gyro in every garage.

205
00:15:56,305 --> 00:15:58,720
Harold Pitcairn built some

206
00:15:58,721 --> 00:16:03,880
autogyros here in the United States back in the mid-1930s, which was right in the middle of the

207
00:16:03,881 --> 00:16:08,200
Great Depression, and nobody really had money for anything at that time.

208
00:16:08,201 --> 00:16:09,280
Unfortunately, the

209
00:16:09,281 --> 00:16:16,900
autogyro, combined with its complexity and high cost, never allowed it to actually be that

210
00:16:16,901 --> 00:16:17,876
competitive.

211
00:16:17,877 --> 00:16:24,300
The autogyro's fate was sealed when in December 1940, its 41-year-old inventor,

212
00:16:24,301 --> 00:16:28,820
Juan de la Sierra was killed in a commuter airplane accident in London.

213
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:36,400
But by solving many of the autogyro's problems,

214
00:16:36,640 --> 00:16:41,700
de la Sierra and his colleagues had opened the way at last for the practical helicopter.

215
00:16:42,560 --> 00:16:44,700
The world wouldn't have to wait long.

216
00:16:45,180 --> 00:16:50,680
Two men working independently of each other were about to push helicopters into the modern era.

217
00:16:50,681 --> 00:17:01,900
On April 22, 1931, Amelia Earhart set a world's altitude record for auto gyros at 18,400 feet.

218
00:17:02,720 --> 00:17:06,500
Helicopters will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

219
00:17:11,160 --> 00:17:15,540
By the middle 1930s, there had been many complicated machines

220
00:17:15,541 --> 00:17:19,000
built in hopes of achieving controlled vertical flight.

221
00:17:19,001 --> 00:17:22,200
Few would ever do more than scoot across the ground.

222
00:17:23,300 --> 00:17:28,700
But in 1936, a German airplane engineer, Heinrich Volcker,

223
00:17:29,100 --> 00:17:31,420
had gone beyond conventional thinking.

224
00:17:32,260 --> 00:17:34,340
Why go to an elaborate new machine?

225
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:38,400
He felt that the trusty autogyro was so close,

226
00:17:38,740 --> 00:17:41,280
he would simply build upon its basic design.

227
00:17:41,281 --> 00:17:53,700
Professor Heinrich Focke was a very brilliant aerodynamicist, a very, very great pioneer,

228
00:17:54,700 --> 00:18:01,460
who started with the autogyro and then built up his know-how of rotary wings and the lifting

229
00:18:01,461 --> 00:18:03,388
and the vibrations and the capabilities.

230
00:18:03,389 --> 00:18:06,040
And from there, it was almost a natural step for him in

231
00:18:06,041 --> 00:18:14,040
1935, 1936, to attack the problem of the helicopter, which he very successfully attacked.

232
00:18:14,700 --> 00:18:17,040
It resulted in the building of two small helicopters.

233
00:18:18,340 --> 00:18:23,240
The two main rotors of the Flock helicopter were side by side.

234
00:18:23,360 --> 00:18:25,300
There was one on the left, one on the right.

235
00:18:26,360 --> 00:18:31,180
And by rotating in opposite directions, one counteracted the torque of the other.

236
00:18:31,181 --> 00:18:35,260
and it had a small propeller in the front that looked like an airplane propeller,

237
00:18:35,380 --> 00:18:37,140
but was actually just used to cool the engine.

238
00:18:37,800 --> 00:18:39,180
But it was a true helicopter.

239
00:18:40,880 --> 00:18:44,560
Heinrich Falka had built the world's first real helicopter.

240
00:18:45,140 --> 00:18:47,240
Although it was difficult to fly and maneuver,

241
00:18:47,660 --> 00:18:51,720
it could hover, fly sideways, and fly forward or backward.

242
00:18:52,400 --> 00:18:57,200
But for all the amazing feats that Falka's F-A61 helicopter achieved,

243
00:18:57,201 --> 00:19:02,440
it had little impact on a public already familiar with airplanes and auto gyros.

244
00:19:03,120 --> 00:19:07,300
That is, until the attractive and daring 25-year-old Hannah Reich,

245
00:19:07,800 --> 00:19:13,160
known throughout Germany as that nation's first woman test pilot, climbed into the cockpit.

246
00:19:14,380 --> 00:19:16,380
Hannah Reich was a very gifted test pilot.

247
00:19:16,780 --> 00:19:23,760
A very, very tiny, tiny little lady, probably weighing 98 pounds, sopping wet,

248
00:19:24,320 --> 00:19:26,460
which was very good for improving the performance.

249
00:19:26,461 --> 00:19:30,860
He didn't want a big tank of a guy climbing into an aircraft which had marginal performance.

250
00:19:31,340 --> 00:19:36,280
Her flights and her demonstration flights were highly publicized,

251
00:19:36,800 --> 00:19:40,660
and those public demonstrations, I think, were pivotal to the cause of the helicopter.

252
00:19:42,220 --> 00:19:50,780
In February 1938, Reich demonstrated the Falka helicopter inside Berlin's cavernous Deutschlandhalle sports arena

253
00:19:50,781 --> 00:19:52,700
every night for three weeks.

254
00:19:52,701 --> 00:20:02,640
She flew from a court about the size of a basketball court and would take off, fly, hover, turn 360 degrees.

255
00:20:02,820 --> 00:20:10,380
And when the films reached the United States, I think they also created a tremendous amount of interest in the helicopter here.

256
00:20:11,500 --> 00:20:15,980
And Hannah Reich, interestingly enough, is a member of an organization that I'm a member of,

257
00:20:16,040 --> 00:20:21,420
which is the International Women Helicopter Pilots, more commonly or better known as the Whirly Girls.

258
00:20:21,421 --> 00:20:27,620
And because Hannah Wright was the first woman to ever pilot a helicopter, she's considered Whirly Girl number one.

259
00:20:28,380 --> 00:20:35,300
And presently, we have over a thousand members of the Whirly Girls, and all of them must be rated pilots.

260
00:20:35,740 --> 00:20:37,840
And they're from 28 different countries.

261
00:20:38,560 --> 00:20:43,440
As Hannah Wright continued to break virtually every helicopter world's record,

262
00:20:44,020 --> 00:20:50,480
an American-Russian immigrant, Igor Sikorsky, was about to change the course of helicopter history.

263
00:20:51,420 --> 00:20:57,700
Igor Sikorsky was no stranger to aeronautics.

264
00:20:57,701 --> 00:21:05,100
By the mid-1920s, he had built one of the most successful airplane companies in the world, Sikorsky Aviation.

265
00:21:05,880 --> 00:21:10,280
In 1929, the company became a subsidiary of United Aircraft,

266
00:21:10,700 --> 00:21:14,860
giving Sikorsky time to pursue a lifelong dream, helicopters.

267
00:21:14,861 --> 00:21:22,080
As a boy in Russia in 1909, Sikorsky had built several unsuccessful helicopters.

268
00:21:22,580 --> 00:21:26,220
But by 1931, he was ready to tackle the task again.

269
00:21:26,980 --> 00:21:32,200
Dad got back into helicopters in the very early 1930s.

270
00:21:33,060 --> 00:21:39,100
At that time, you could say his work, nine to five, were the large clipper ships,

271
00:21:39,240 --> 00:21:43,080
the flying boats for Pan American Airways and for a number of other airlines.

272
00:21:43,081 --> 00:21:52,240
But already in 1931, 32, 33, you begin to see the sketches increasing in volume, the sketches of a helicopter.

273
00:21:52,980 --> 00:22:00,500
And by 1935, 1936 already, I was building small little models that he would then demonstrate to his engineers

274
00:22:00,501 --> 00:22:05,400
or take up to the board of directors of United Aircraft, as it was called in those days.

275
00:22:06,700 --> 00:22:10,720
Sikorsky's sketches resulted in a unique design for the time period.

276
00:22:10,721 --> 00:22:12,460
a single main rotor.

277
00:22:12,920 --> 00:22:17,440
Up until the late 1930s, most helicopters that had lifted off the ground

278
00:22:17,441 --> 00:22:22,040
were either coaxial in design or had at least two main rotors,

279
00:22:22,360 --> 00:22:23,900
such as the Falka helicopter.

280
00:22:24,980 --> 00:22:31,500
A number of people had tried to build a single main lifting rotor machine,

281
00:22:32,160 --> 00:22:36,340
but actually none of them were successful until that time.

282
00:22:36,341 --> 00:22:40,600
And in fact, many authorities considered the single main lifting rotor machine

283
00:22:40,601 --> 00:22:43,600
to be technically and theoretically impossible.

284
00:22:44,140 --> 00:22:45,200
It just could not be done.

285
00:22:46,180 --> 00:22:50,340
However, by 1936-1938, Igor Sikorsky had zeroed in

286
00:22:50,341 --> 00:22:52,660
and sat down on this concept

287
00:22:52,661 --> 00:22:55,500
and was beginning to develop the necessary hardware,

288
00:22:55,720 --> 00:22:59,000
the necessary design philosophy for a single rotor machine.

289
00:23:00,120 --> 00:23:05,260
On September 14, 1939, Sikorsky began cautious flight tests

290
00:23:05,261 --> 00:23:07,720
in his single main rotor VS-300.

291
00:23:08,500 --> 00:23:13,760
He kept his craft tethered to the ground while he slowly worked the kinks out of his design.

292
00:23:15,400 --> 00:23:21,600
Concentrating heavily on how to control his craft, Sikorsky focused on a rear tail rotor configuration.

293
00:23:22,220 --> 00:23:26,520
He tried everything from no tail rotor to three tail rotors.

294
00:23:27,220 --> 00:23:33,120
He was trying to obtain some of the other control movements of the helicopter with these other rotors.

295
00:23:33,121 --> 00:23:44,895
He finally got rid of the rest of the other ones and ended up with a single tail rotor back there to counteract torque and also to give the pilot control of rotating

296
00:23:44,896 --> 00:23:48,820
the helicopter to the left or rotating it to the right.

297
00:23:49,400 --> 00:23:54,680
So the tail rotor really performs both of those functions, counteracting torque and providing directional control.

298
00:23:54,681 --> 00:24:00,940
The tail rotor was, and it still is, with modern helicopters, controlled with foot pedals.

299
00:24:00,941 --> 00:24:06,540
When the pilot wants to turn the helicopter to the left, he pushes the left pedal in,

300
00:24:06,780 --> 00:24:09,500
which decreases the angle of the rear rotor blades,

301
00:24:09,860 --> 00:24:13,900
allowing the natural forces of torque to turn the fuselage to the left.

302
00:24:14,560 --> 00:24:18,400
Pushing in the right pedal increases the pitch of the rear rotor blades,

303
00:24:18,720 --> 00:24:23,060
causing them to bite the air harder and overcompensates the forces of torque,

304
00:24:23,360 --> 00:24:25,300
and the aircraft swings to the right.

305
00:24:25,301 --> 00:24:31,260
but for Igor Sikorsky in 1940

306
00:24:31,261 --> 00:24:34,660
creating this design concept did not happen overnight

307
00:24:34,661 --> 00:24:40,340
there were many flight tests that would demand numerous modifications

308
00:24:40,341 --> 00:24:45,860
so many that Sikorsky's mechanics affectionately nicknamed the VS-300

309
00:24:45,861 --> 00:24:48,200
as Igor's nightmare

310
00:24:48,201 --> 00:24:53,740
the memories are very vivid of the very early flights of VS-300

311
00:24:53,741 --> 00:25:00,280
those benign, beautiful, warm spring days of 1940

312
00:25:00,281 --> 00:25:06,920
as the helicopter would be hovering just in a parking lot behind the Sikorsky factory.

313
00:25:06,921 --> 00:25:12,260
And I remember you could touch it the way you could touch a living thing,

314
00:25:12,420 --> 00:25:14,480
like a horse or a dog.

315
00:25:15,040 --> 00:25:17,120
And you had that affection for it.

316
00:25:17,620 --> 00:25:22,620
Igor Sikorsky's VS-300 was America's first successful helicopter.

317
00:25:23,740 --> 00:25:29,100
His design and patent became the basis for future modern helicopters.

318
00:25:30,100 --> 00:25:35,800
Army Air Force at that time became very interested in the aircraft

319
00:25:35,801 --> 00:25:41,220
and gave them $50,000 to finish the development of that aircraft.

320
00:25:41,560 --> 00:25:44,060
United Aircraft, of course, added a lot of money to that,

321
00:25:44,520 --> 00:25:48,580
and they developed then, under Air Force contract, the XR-4.

322
00:25:48,581 --> 00:25:54,440
And that's the aircraft that's sitting behind me is the first-in-production aircraft.

323
00:25:55,060 --> 00:25:59,920
This is the only remaining prototype of the XR-4.

324
00:26:00,420 --> 00:26:06,880
By the end of World War II, over 400 Sikorsky helicopters had rolled off the assembly lines.

325
00:26:08,480 --> 00:26:12,100
Military leaders first thought of the helicopter as a scouting tool.

326
00:26:12,600 --> 00:26:16,140
But the helicopter quickly proved to be capable of much more.

327
00:26:16,800 --> 00:26:18,820
During the last years of World War II,

328
00:26:19,380 --> 00:26:24,120
many stranded victims were rescued in spots that only the helicopter could reach.

329
00:26:24,740 --> 00:26:29,280
The military was impressed with the life-saving potential of this new machine.

330
00:26:30,640 --> 00:26:34,640
The helicopter had shown that it could be a truly useful aircraft,

331
00:26:35,040 --> 00:26:37,060
a rotary-winged Angel of Mercy.

332
00:26:37,061 --> 00:26:43,020
In years to come, it would be that and more, in peace as well as war.

333
00:26:43,880 --> 00:26:50,560
The first helicopter hoist rescue took place in November 1945 with the Sikorsky R-5.

334
00:26:51,320 --> 00:26:56,040
The helicopter pulled two seamen from a sinking barge off the coast of Connecticut.

335
00:26:57,420 --> 00:27:01,120
Helicopters will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

336
00:27:03,880 --> 00:27:08,160
Early helicopter pioneers all had one thing in common.

337
00:27:08,760 --> 00:27:11,740
There wasn't much of a technological track record to follow.

338
00:27:11,741 --> 00:27:16,740
Much of the early designs were based on intuition.

339
00:27:16,741 --> 00:27:22,740
Well, use your best judgment and your imagination

340
00:27:22,741 --> 00:27:29,740
and do what you think you have to do to conceive and develop a system that will work.

341
00:27:29,741 --> 00:27:38,740
If it worked, you made it like we did 55 years ago on the 11th of April 1943.

342
00:27:38,741 --> 00:27:46,640
Frank Piasecki built and flew America's second successful helicopter, the Piasecki PV-2.

343
00:27:47,720 --> 00:27:55,700
Just like Igor Sikorsky before him, Piasecki's first flights in a helicopter were tethered to the ground for safety.

344
00:27:56,560 --> 00:28:07,400
It was a kind of windy day, but we started the machine up and we had my mother's clotheslines I borrowed to hold the machine to the ground.

345
00:28:08,740 --> 00:28:14,840
And all of a sudden, a gust of wind came along lifting me up off the ground, broke the ropes.

346
00:28:15,940 --> 00:28:17,360
Everybody ran away.

347
00:28:18,540 --> 00:28:30,500
And just by some stroke of the Lord, I moved my hand in the right direction and countered the turn that the wind did.

348
00:28:30,740 --> 00:28:33,120
And came back on the ground and stopped.

349
00:28:34,380 --> 00:28:35,760
The machine was saved.

350
00:28:36,980 --> 00:28:38,680
And that was our first takeoff.

351
00:28:38,740 --> 00:28:45,740
Piasecki's PV-2 helicopter evolved into a fully controllable aircraft.

352
00:28:45,741 --> 00:28:51,740
Just like the other early helicopter pioneers, Piasecki's machine took months to perfect.

353
00:28:51,741 --> 00:28:59,740
Not only were engineers dealing with the unknown, but with helicopter technology being so complex and so new,

354
00:28:59,741 --> 00:29:03,740
gathering support for such endeavors wasn't always easy.

355
00:29:03,741 --> 00:29:09,260
If you said, I'm designing a helicopter, people would look upon you as a nut.

356
00:29:09,500 --> 00:29:16,040
It was known that it had a lot of failures by many different people and many celebrated engineers.

357
00:29:17,040 --> 00:29:22,860
And therefore, who the hell are you that you're going to design a helicopter that will fly?

358
00:29:23,540 --> 00:29:25,200
So get out of my way.

359
00:29:25,700 --> 00:29:29,720
But by the time the clouds of World War II began to part,

360
00:29:29,721 --> 00:29:36,280
More than 50 companies were working at helicopter development, all hoping for a governmental contract.

361
00:29:36,720 --> 00:29:40,540
It was Frank Piasecki who got the attention of the Navy.

362
00:29:42,120 --> 00:29:51,320
Frank Piasecki designed a helicopter that had two rotors, but instead of being a lateral twin, meaning that it had rotors on each side,

363
00:29:51,321 --> 00:29:59,180
he put one of the main rotors at the forward end of the helicopter and the other main rotor at the aft end of the helicopter.

364
00:29:59,720 --> 00:30:05,020
Again, they rotated in opposite directions so that each one compensated for the torque reaction of the other.

365
00:30:06,880 --> 00:30:14,320
The Navy then gave us an order for 10 of them because they wanted to try out a squadron of them.

366
00:30:14,321 --> 00:30:21,120
And with those 10, they were able to apply this new thing that they had,

367
00:30:21,200 --> 00:30:28,360
which gave them 1,800 pounds of vertical lift, which gave them more speed.

368
00:30:28,361 --> 00:30:34,420
which gave them more maneuverability, towing things for instance, mine sweeping.

369
00:30:36,180 --> 00:30:40,100
Piasseki built several variations of tandem rotor helicopters.

370
00:30:40,880 --> 00:30:45,160
Shortly after World War II, they were in use with the Navy, Marines and Coast Guard.

371
00:30:45,980 --> 00:30:53,420
However, by August 1950, the armed forces found themselves in desperate need for hundreds of new helicopters

372
00:30:53,421 --> 00:30:56,220
in a war that no one expected.

373
00:30:58,360 --> 00:31:05,360
without a doubt the korean war

374
00:31:06,660 --> 00:31:07,920
convinced

375
00:31:07,921 --> 00:31:09,440
all of the skeptics

376
00:31:09,441 --> 00:31:12,900
because within a few months after the start of the war

377
00:31:12,901 --> 00:31:16,300
the helicopter had gone to almost priority number one

378
00:31:16,301 --> 00:31:19,300
it was used for medical evacuation

379
00:31:19,301 --> 00:31:26,300
it began to be used for the rescue of pilots down behind enemy lines

380
00:31:26,301 --> 00:31:35,240
At first, the majority of the helicopters in use were Frank Piasecki's PV-3s, more commonly known as flying bananas.

381
00:31:38,640 --> 00:31:42,880
Also in use were Igor Sikorsky's S-51 helicopters.

382
00:31:43,780 --> 00:31:48,100
But for medical evacuation, a smaller, lightweight helicopter was needed.

383
00:31:48,101 --> 00:31:58,260
The design came from a brilliant young inventor, Arthur Young, who worked for the famous Bell Aircraft Corporation.

384
00:31:59,460 --> 00:32:04,080
Young had built America's third successful helicopter, the Bell Model 30.

385
00:32:04,660 --> 00:32:13,220
This craft first flew on June 26, 1943, only two months after Piasecki had lifted off the ground for the first time.

386
00:32:13,221 --> 00:32:24,660
Yet two years later, after an evolution of refinements and improvements, Arthur Young churned out the Bell 47 helicopter.

387
00:32:25,740 --> 00:32:31,120
The Bell 47 became the symbol of hope for American soldiers during the Korean War.

388
00:32:31,980 --> 00:32:37,280
They soon became an integral part of MASH units, or Mobile Army Surgical Hospitals.

389
00:32:37,281 --> 00:32:47,620
With its bulbous plastic cockpit, the Bell 47 quickly became one of the most recognizable helicopters in the world.

390
00:32:48,480 --> 00:32:54,200
Its characteristic clop-clop-clop-clop sound resulted in the coined term chopper.

391
00:32:54,201 --> 00:33:04,800
and by the end of the Korean War, I believe the helicopter had evacuated well over 100,000 wounded troops

392
00:33:04,801 --> 00:33:14,180
and had what is equally significant, had rescued and brought to safety something like 1,200 pilots from behind enemy lines.

393
00:33:14,840 --> 00:33:21,560
Men that would have faced capture, starvation, torture and probably death if it had not been for the helicopter.

394
00:33:21,561 --> 00:33:25,740
In 1953, the Korean War was over.

395
00:33:26,320 --> 00:33:30,140
But helicopters would soon become more than scout and rescue machines.

396
00:33:30,680 --> 00:33:34,820
They would become a viable military weapon in a distant conflict.

397
00:33:35,100 --> 00:33:37,960
It would become known as the Helicopter War.

398
00:33:39,460 --> 00:33:44,820
However, harmony between helicopters and the military in Vietnam would take a little time.

399
00:33:46,940 --> 00:33:50,280
Virtually all helicopters utilized piston engines,

400
00:33:50,281 --> 00:33:54,560
which relative to their size and weight were bulky and weak.

401
00:33:55,200 --> 00:34:00,500
When enlarged, they took up too much space and could barely lift themselves and a large payload.

402
00:34:01,260 --> 00:34:04,380
The military quickly turned to the turbine engine,

403
00:34:04,880 --> 00:34:09,380
a source of power that throughout the 1950s was still in the experimental stages.

404
00:34:10,020 --> 00:34:14,180
But by the 1960s, after a series of refinements and improvements,

405
00:34:14,680 --> 00:34:16,980
the turbine engine was in mass production.

406
00:34:16,981 --> 00:34:23,760
The turbines were not introduced into helicopters until the mid to late 50s.

407
00:34:25,100 --> 00:34:28,160
And by the time the Vietnam War had gotten into full swing,

408
00:34:28,540 --> 00:34:34,640
the military had converted most of its helicopters to turbine engines.

409
00:34:35,340 --> 00:34:38,000
And again, those helicopters were all fairly large,

410
00:34:38,380 --> 00:34:43,500
and the turbine engine functions very well in helicopters of that size.

411
00:34:43,501 --> 00:34:47,140
gives them a much better power to weight ratio.

412
00:34:48,340 --> 00:34:53,600
A turbine engine has a spinning fan that sucks air into a combustion chamber

413
00:34:53,601 --> 00:34:56,300
where it is mixed with fuel and ignited.

414
00:34:58,180 --> 00:35:04,120
As the fuel burns, it produces hot gases which shoot into the turbine causing it to spin.

415
00:35:05,060 --> 00:35:10,320
Attached is a shaft that turns the rotor and any other devices connected to it,

416
00:35:10,321 --> 00:35:15,680
such as gearboxes for the rear tail rotor and power generators, etc.

417
00:35:16,680 --> 00:35:21,320
A reciprocating engine, for the horsepower it delivers,

418
00:35:21,560 --> 00:35:26,420
its weight to horsepower ratio is generally about equal.

419
00:35:26,880 --> 00:35:32,840
So, I mean, if you had a 400 horsepower engine, it's going to weigh about 400 pounds.

420
00:35:33,400 --> 00:35:35,440
In a turbine engine, it's about half.

421
00:35:35,441 --> 00:35:39,140
If you have a 400 horsepower engine, it's going to weigh about 200 pounds.

422
00:35:39,141 --> 00:35:42,480
And a helicopter, just like any aircraft, weight is precious.

423
00:35:43,520 --> 00:35:48,680
It was a turbine-driven helicopter that became the symbol of the Vietnam War.

424
00:35:49,860 --> 00:35:53,660
The Bell, HU-1, more commonly known as the Huey,

425
00:35:54,180 --> 00:35:58,880
a nickname the Army pilots came up with based on the helicopter's initials.

426
00:36:00,020 --> 00:36:03,520
My first few months in Vietnam was flying Hueys.

427
00:36:03,720 --> 00:36:07,400
I think the Huey was the most versatile helicopter in the Vietnam War.

428
00:36:07,401 --> 00:36:10,720
and like I said, I think it became one of the most versatile helicopters ever built

429
00:36:10,721 --> 00:36:13,160
just by its pure design and its capabilities.

430
00:36:13,400 --> 00:36:17,180
Easy to train pilots for, take enough troops, a squad say.

431
00:36:17,620 --> 00:36:19,980
You could put hard mounts on the outside of the aircraft

432
00:36:19,981 --> 00:36:22,620
and turn it into a gun platform if it had to be.

433
00:36:22,680 --> 00:36:25,460
You could put litters inside and use it as an air ambulance.

434
00:36:25,880 --> 00:36:29,420
You could take an aircraft and configure it very quickly

435
00:36:29,421 --> 00:36:32,040
for three or four or five different kind of missions.

436
00:36:32,700 --> 00:36:37,220
But flying these machines in war exposed both pilot and aircraft

437
00:36:37,221 --> 00:36:38,214
to great risk.

438
00:36:38,215 --> 00:36:43,820
While rescuing wounded soldiers, often from the front lines, helicopters became

439
00:36:43,821 --> 00:36:46,727
large moving air targets for the enemy.

440
00:36:46,728 --> 00:36:50,900
There was really nothing really bulletproof about a Huey

441
00:36:50,901 --> 00:36:57,400
other than that it was a pretty simple design and there was a few critical components and if they

442
00:36:57,401 --> 00:36:59,654
were hit, it would knock it out of the sky.

443
00:36:59,655 --> 00:37:02,380
There was a lot of helicopters lost in Vietnam and a

444
00:37:02,381 --> 00:37:05,520
A lot of helicopter pilots lost their lives in Vietnam

445
00:37:05,521 --> 00:37:07,720
because of just the pure exposure,

446
00:37:07,721 --> 00:37:09,820
just the amount of hours that were flown.

447
00:37:10,880 --> 00:37:12,760
But there was not a more welcome sight

448
00:37:12,761 --> 00:37:15,220
to American troops fighting in flooded paddies

449
00:37:15,221 --> 00:37:18,800
and look-alike villages than that of the Bell HU-1.

450
00:37:20,040 --> 00:37:23,540
The Huey was looked upon as an angel of mercy,

451
00:37:23,541 --> 00:37:26,740
one of the most respected machines of that war.

452
00:37:26,741 --> 00:37:29,740
I think of the Huey affectionately

453
00:37:29,741 --> 00:37:31,680
because we were raised with it.

454
00:37:31,681 --> 00:37:34,880
In fact, you get a little emotional just thinking about it.

455
00:37:35,080 --> 00:37:37,780
That so classic of a Huey.

456
00:37:38,080 --> 00:37:42,520
I went from being an apprehensive manipulator of the controls

457
00:37:42,521 --> 00:37:45,460
to becoming a helicopter pilot in the Huey.

458
00:37:45,560 --> 00:37:47,600
And I think I'll just always remember that.

459
00:37:47,800 --> 00:37:52,760
I'm very fortunate that our department operates a surplus Huey.

460
00:37:52,761 --> 00:37:54,660
And I occasionally get to fly it.

461
00:37:55,160 --> 00:37:58,060
And I hope it's the last helicopter I fly.

462
00:37:58,061 --> 00:38:01,780
I'll always have a soft spot in my heart for the Huey.

463
00:38:02,140 --> 00:38:09,580
By the end of the Vietnam conflict, the helicopter had proven itself as an efficient and ubiquitous servant in war.

464
00:38:09,960 --> 00:38:19,040
But soon, the world would see that the most dramatic helicopter duty remained the same in peace as in war, rescue.

465
00:38:19,041 --> 00:38:31,160
Less than 20 years after the birth of the modern helicopter, the world became reliant

466
00:38:31,161 --> 00:38:34,040
upon its existence.

467
00:38:34,041 --> 00:38:40,660
I think certainly to the army of the United States, and I would dare say to the armies

468
00:38:40,661 --> 00:38:46,720
of a number of military services around the world, the helicopter certainly is as indispensable

469
00:38:46,721 --> 00:38:50,200
as the jet fighter is to the air forces of the world.

470
00:38:51,000 --> 00:38:56,280
It does provide you simply with the capability to continue military operations

471
00:38:56,281 --> 00:39:00,280
in areas that would be inaccessible with anything but the helicopter.

472
00:39:01,200 --> 00:39:07,340
Currently, the latest in helicopter technology can be found in some of the various military machines,

473
00:39:07,840 --> 00:39:10,220
such as the Boeing Sikorsky Comanche.

474
00:39:11,000 --> 00:39:15,600
This stealth machine can fly at speeds up to 200 miles per hour

475
00:39:15,601 --> 00:39:19,560
and execute snap turns in 4.5 seconds.

476
00:39:20,660 --> 00:39:24,700
The Comanche can fly sideways or backwards at 70 miles per hour

477
00:39:24,701 --> 00:39:29,200
while the pilot utilizes the craft's advanced radar and infrared sensors.

478
00:39:29,840 --> 00:39:34,220
In seconds, the Comanche's computers can scan miles of a battlefield,

479
00:39:34,960 --> 00:39:40,980
instantly prioritize all hostile targets, including tanks, trucks, and even other helicopters.

480
00:39:41,680 --> 00:39:44,360
While displaying the targets for crew identification,

481
00:39:44,361 --> 00:39:48,340
the system selects the optimum weapon to neutralize each,

482
00:39:48,800 --> 00:39:51,180
either from its own extensive weapon systems

483
00:39:51,181 --> 00:39:55,860
or by relaying encrypted information to other air or ground commanders.

484
00:39:59,460 --> 00:40:04,460
But the civilian side of helicopter technology is no less important.

485
00:40:05,200 --> 00:40:09,520
Helicopters are always on standby for our safety and benefit.

486
00:40:10,400 --> 00:40:13,900
A lot of people don't realize one of the special functions of a helicopter

487
00:40:13,901 --> 00:40:21,000
in a large metropolitan area is when the corporate helicopters are members of a disaster relief program.

488
00:40:21,620 --> 00:40:25,560
And in the New York City area, for example, all the corporate helicopters that fly around,

489
00:40:25,620 --> 00:40:30,900
not only just fly the executives around to save time and bring in a lot of business to the area,

490
00:40:30,980 --> 00:40:35,120
which is very important for the economy, but should an unexpected disaster occur,

491
00:40:35,121 --> 00:40:39,480
for example, some kind of a bombing or just some kind of a national disaster,

492
00:40:39,640 --> 00:40:42,480
the helicopters would all rally in support of the community.

493
00:40:42,481 --> 00:40:46,500
And this is something that we do in addition, just as a community service.

494
00:40:47,660 --> 00:40:54,500
Today I think we can say as a rough ballpark figure that well over one and a half million human lives

495
00:40:54,501 --> 00:40:59,800
directly saved by the helicopter, by the intervention of the helicopter.

496
00:41:01,400 --> 00:41:08,700
In 1957, President Eisenhower took a ride in a Bell H-13J from the White House lawn

497
00:41:08,701 --> 00:41:12,960
to a command post as part of a civil defense exercise,

498
00:41:13,480 --> 00:41:16,520
becoming the first president to fly in a helicopter.

499
00:41:17,800 --> 00:41:19,480
Modern marvels will return.

500
00:41:20,840 --> 00:41:23,720
Helicopters also act as aerial firefighters.

501
00:41:24,400 --> 00:41:27,920
They can access much smaller water supplies than tanker planes.

502
00:41:28,680 --> 00:41:34,080
These monster Ericsson air cranes can quickly suck up over 2,000 gallons of water

503
00:41:34,081 --> 00:41:39,320
and then with precise accuracy dispense the water over the affected area.

504
00:41:42,400 --> 00:41:48,300
Without these air cranes, many industrial projects would cost millions of dollars more to build.

505
00:41:48,880 --> 00:41:51,720
There is no need to impact the environment with roads

506
00:41:51,721 --> 00:41:56,180
as these heavy lift Ericsson helicopters aid in power line construction,

507
00:41:58,660 --> 00:41:59,960
remote logging,

508
00:41:59,961 --> 00:42:05,700
and major equipment transportation.

509
00:42:07,260 --> 00:42:13,800
This unit is one of the first unmanned helicopters being developed for law enforcement and the military.

510
00:42:14,840 --> 00:42:18,940
Utilizing a single main rotor protected in the middle of the aircraft,

511
00:42:18,941 --> 00:42:22,840
this Sikorsky rotorcraft performs a variety of functions,

512
00:42:23,220 --> 00:42:28,820
including capturing live footage and transmitting it back to a safely isolated base station.

513
00:42:29,960 --> 00:42:34,960
Unmanned helicopters may one day be a common sight.

514
00:42:34,961 --> 00:42:38,960
Oh, the future of helicopters is absolutely exciting.

515
00:42:38,961 --> 00:42:41,960
Just a branch off from the helicopter is the tilt rotor,

516
00:42:41,961 --> 00:42:47,960
and that's where I think that you're going to mesh the vertical flight and horizontal flight.

517
00:42:47,961 --> 00:42:50,960
In fact, what it is is the tilt rotor is the best of both worlds.

518
00:42:50,961 --> 00:42:55,960
You can land and take off like a helicopter and have the speed and range of an airplane.

519
00:42:59,960 --> 00:43:06,020
Man's thirst for perfecting three-dimensional flight appears to be an unfinished quest.

520
00:43:06,720 --> 00:43:13,760
But whatever we achieve in the future, the helicopter will always remain our source of inspiration.

521
00:43:14,900 --> 00:43:24,100
But I think the most special thing about the helicopter is the fact that it allowed man to live what had been considered up to now a dream.

522
00:43:24,101 --> 00:43:30,040
The ability to take off and land vertically and the ability to hover motionless in the air

523
00:43:30,041 --> 00:43:33,440
is something that literally only the helicopter can give mankind.

524
00:43:33,780 --> 00:43:39,060
My father, Igor Sikorsky, would often say that it's interesting that all of the great early legends of flight,

525
00:43:39,840 --> 00:43:46,540
the Pegasus, the flying carpets, the flying thrones of the Chinese, all of these had one thing in common.

526
00:43:47,140 --> 00:43:51,660
They did not need an airport. These were all vertical takeoff and landing machines.

527
00:43:51,661 --> 00:43:55,660
So that is what I consider to be the uniqueness of the helicopter.

